===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|legal}} A writ summoning one to answer; an official letter or [[mandate]].
## {{sid|en|papal brief}} {{lb|en|Roman Catholicism}} A short [[papal]] [[letter]].
##: {{cot|en|bull<id:papal bull><qq:longer>}}
# {{lb|en|legal}} An answer to any action.
# {{lb|en|legal}} A memorandum of points of fact or of law for use in conducting a case.
# {{lb|en|by extension|figurative}} A position of interest or [[advocacy]].
# {{lb|en|legal}} An attorney's legal [[argument]] in [[written]] form for submission to a court.
# {{lb|en|English law}} The material relevant to a case, delivered by a solicitor to the barrister who is [[counsel]] for the case.
# {{lb|en|English law|slang}} A barrister who is counsel for a party in a legal action.
# A [[short]] [[news]] story or [[report]].
#: {{ux|en|We got a news '''brief'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 4-1|act=IV|scene=iv|passage=Hie good ſir Mighell, beare this ſealed '''briefe'''&nbsp;/ With winged haſte to the Lord Marſhall&nbsp;/ This to my cooſen Scroope, and all the reſt&nbsp;/ To whom they are directed.}}
# {{lb|en|usually|in the plural}} [[underwear|Underwear]] [[briefs]].
#: {{ux|en|I wear boxer shorts under trousers but for sports I usually wear a '''brief'''.}}
# {{attributive form of|en|briefs}}.
# {{lb|en|usually|in the plural}} [[swimming briefs|Swimming briefs]].
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} A [[summary]], [[précis]] or [[epitome]]; an [[abridgement]] or [[abstract]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1589|author=Thomas Nashe|title=The Anatomie of Absurditie|url=https://archive.org/stream/cu31924013133727#page/n92
|passage={{...}} euen ſo it fareth with mee, who béeing about to anatomize Abſurditie, am vrged to take a view of ſundry mens vanitie, a ſuruey of their follie, a '''briefe''' of their barbariſme {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=a. 1613|author=Sir {{w|Thomas Overbury}}|chapter=A Wife|title=The Miscellaneous Works in Verse and Prose of Sir Thomas Overbury, Knt. with Memoir of his Life|edition=10th|location=London|publisher=W. Owen|year_published=1756|page=23|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=tdoxAQAAMAAJ&pg=PA49|passage=EACH woman is a '''brief''' of womankind, / And doth in little e’en as much contain, / As in one day and night all life we find&nbsp;; / Of either more is but the ſame again {{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|UK|historical}} A [[letter patent]], from proper authority, authorizing a [[collection]] or [[charitable]] [[contribution]] of [[money]] in [[church]]es, for any public or private purpose.
# {{lb|en|slang}} A [[ticket]] of any type.<ref>The Routledge Dictionary of Historical Slang</ref><ref>John Camden Hotten's ''Slang Dictionary'' (1873)</ref>

