====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[come#Verb|come]] or [[go#Verb|go]] [[near#Adverb|near]], in [[place#Noun|place]] or [[time#Noun|time]]; to move toward; to [[advance#Verb|advance]] nearer; to [[draw#Verb|draw]] [[nigh#Adverb|nigh]].
#: {{ux|en|She '''approached''' the door cautiously.}}
#: {{ux|en|We were '''approached''' by a stranger asking for directions.}}
#: {{ux|en|The deadline is fast '''approaching'''.}} 
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare King Lear Q1|act=II|scene=ii|page=45|passage='''Approach''' thou beacon [the Sun] to this vnder gloabe, <br> That by thy comfortable beames I may <br> Peruſe this letter, nothing almoſt ſees my wracke <br> But miſerie.}}
#* {{RQ:King James Version|2 Samuel|11|20|page=417|column=2|passage=And if ſo be that the kings wrath ariſe, and hee ſay vnto thee, wherefore '''approched''' ye ſo nigh vnto the city when yee did fight? Knew yee not that they would ſhoot from the wall?}}
#* {{RQ:King James Version|Hebrews|10|24–25|page=1466|column=1|passage=And let vs conſider one another to prouoke vnto loue, and to good workes: Not forſaking the aſſembling of ourſelues together, as the manner of ſome is: but exhorting one another, and ſo much the more, as ye ſee the day '''approching'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Dickens Christmas Carol|pages=35–36|pageref=35|passage=It beckoned Scrooge to '''approach''', which he did. When they were within two paces of each other, Marley's Ghost held up its hand, warning him to come no nearer. Scrooge stopped.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|golf|tennis}} To [[play#Verb|play]] an [[approach shot]].
# {{lb|en|ambitransitive|figuratively}} ''Used intransitively, followed by'' '''[[to]]''': to draw near (to someone or something); to [[make#Verb|make]] [[advance#Noun|advances]]; to [[approximate#Verb|approximate]] or become almost [[equal#Adjective|equal]].
#: {{ux|en|He '''approaches''' to the character of the ablest statesman.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Tristram Merton [pseudonym; [[w:Thomas Babington Macaulay, 1st Baron Macaulay|Thomas Babington Macaulay]]]|title=Criticisms on the Principal Italian Writers. No. I. Dante.|editor=<nowiki>[</nowiki>[[w:Charles Knight (publisher)|Charles Knight]]<nowiki>]</nowiki>|magazine=Knight’s Quarterly Magazine|location=London|publisher={{...|Printed [by]}} <nowiki>[</nowiki>[[w:William Clowes (printer)|William Clowes]]<nowiki>]</nowiki> for Charles Knight,{{nb...|Pall Mall East.}}|month=January|year=1824|volume=II|issue=I|page=215|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=eVNFAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA215|oclc=614542612|passage=The great source, as it appears to me, of the power of the {{w|Divine Comedy}} [by {{w|Dante Alighieri}}], is the strong belief with which the story seems to be told. In this respect, the only books which '''approach''' to its excellence are {{w|Gulliver's Travels}} and {{w|Robinson Crusoe}}.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:Samuel Laing (travel writer)|Samuel Laing]]|chapter=IX|title=A Tour in Sweden in 1838; Comprising Observations on the Moral, Political, and Economical State of the Swedish Nation|location=London|publisher={{...|Printed [by]}} <nowiki>[</nowiki>{{w|Andrew Spottiswoode}}<nowiki>]</nowiki> for [[w:Longman|Longman, Orme, Brown, Green, & Longmans]],{{nb...|Paternoster-Row.}}|year=1839|page=371|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=FGJjAAAAMAAJ&pg=PA371|oclc=27033245|passage=Without these incentives to industry the Norwegian would be like the Laplander, without industry and civilisation; and the nearer he '''approaches''' to the ''beau idéal'' of those political economists—to the state of being without a taste for these foreign and expensive luxuries—the nearer he '''approaches''' to the condition of the Laplander in the comforts and enjoyments of life.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|rarely|intransitive}} Of an [[immovable#Adjective|immovable]] [[object#Noun|object]] or a [[number#Noun|number]] of such objects: to be [[position#Verb|positioned]] as to ([[notionally]]) [[appear]] to be [[move#Verb|moving]] towards (a place).
#: {{ux|en|As we drove along the driveway, the trees '''approaching''' the house seemed more eerie.}}
#* {{RQ:Spectator|author=Addison|issue=477|date=6 September 1712|page=351|passage=[T]here appears a seeming mount, made up of trees rising one higher than another, in proportion as they '''approach''' the centre.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|also|figuratively}} To move toward (someone or something) in place, time, [[character]], or [[value#Noun|value]]; to draw nearer to.
#: {{ux|en|“Would counsel please '''approach''' the bench?” asked the judge.}}
#: {{ux|en|He '''approached''' the age of manhood.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Macbeth|act=II|scene=iii|page=137|column=2|passage='''Approch''' the Chamber, and deſtroy your ſight <br> With a new ''{{w|Gorgon}}''.}}
#* {{RQ:Temple Miscellanea|part=3|page=223|passage=He <nowiki>[</nowiki>{{w|Empedocles}}<nowiki>]</nowiki> was an admirable Poet, and thought even to have '''approached''' ''{{w|Homer}}'', in a Poem he writ of Natural Philoſophy; [...]}}
#* {{RQ:Defoe Crusoe|pages=190–191|pageref=191|passage={{...}} I ſtuck all the Ground without my Wall, for a great way every way, as full with Stakes or Sticks of the ''Osier''-like Wood, which I found ſo apt to grow, as they could well ſtand; inſomuch, that I believe I might ſet in near twenty thouſand of them, leaving a pretty large Space between them and my Wall, that I might have room to ſee an Enemy, and they might have no ſhelter from the young Trees, if they attempted to '''approach''' my outer Wall.}}
#* {{RQ:Audubon Ornithological Biography|chapter=The American Redstart. ''Muscicapa Ruticilla'', Linn.{{nb...|Plate XL. Male and Female.}}|page=234|passage=When one '''approaches''' the nest of this species, the male exhibits the greatest anxiety respecting its safety, passes and repasses, fluttering and snapping its bill within a few feet, as if determined to repel the intruder.}}
#* {{RQ:Dickens Oliver Twist|volume=III|chapter=And Last|page=309|passage=Mr. Brownlow adopted Oliver as his own son, and removing with him and the old housekeeper to within a mile of the parsonage house, where his dear friends resided, he gratified the only remaining wish of Oliver's warm and earnest heart, and thus linked together a little society, whose condition '''approached''' as nearly to one of perfect happiness as can ever be known in this changing world.}}
#* {{RQ:Wells War of the Worlds|chapter=The Eve of the War|page=3|passage=The secular cooling that must some day overtake our planet has already gone far indeed with our neighbour [Mars]. Its physical condition is still largely a mystery, but we know now that even in its equatorial region the mid-day temperature barely '''approaches''' that of our coldest winter.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:William Anthony Granville|chapter=Theory of Limits|editor=[[w:Percey F. Smith|Percey F[ranklyn] Smith]]|title=Elements of the Differential and Integral Calculus|location=Boston, Mass.; New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:Pearson Education|Ginn & Company]]|year=1904|section=paragraph 29 (Limit of a Variable)|page=19|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/elementsdiffere00smitgoog/page/n41/mode/1up|oclc=926065625|passage=If a variable <math>v</math> takes on successively a series of values that '''approach''' nearer and nearer to a constant value <math>l</math> in such a manner that <math>|v - l|</math> [''Footnote'': To be read ''the numerical value of the difference between'' <math>v</math> ''and'' <math>l</math>] becomes and remains less than any assigned arbitrarily small positive quantity, then <math>v</math> is said to '''''approach''' the limit <math>l</math>'', or to ''converge to the limit <math>l</math>''. Symbolically this is written <math>\operatorname{limit} v = l</math>.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To bring (something) near something else; to [[cause#Verb|cause]] (something) to draw near.
#* {{RQ:Scott Kenilworth|volume=I|chapter=X|page=263|passage="My story is not long," said the artist; "but your honour had better sit while you listen to it." So saying, he '''approached''' to the fire a three-footed stool, and took another himself, {{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|analysis}} ''Used when defining [[limit]]s, preceded by'' '''[[as]]''': To become arbitrarily close to some value, be it a [[number]], [[vector]] or [[infinity]] and have an effect on another value. 
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[attempt#Verb|attempt]] to [[make#verb|make]] (a [[policy]]) or [[solve#Verb|solve]] (a [[problem#Noun|problem]]).
#* {{RQ:Fitzgerald Jazz Age|part=II|pages=243–244|pageref=243|passage=Mr. Moonlight Quill, mysterious, exotic, and oriental in temperament was, nevertheless, a man of decision. And it was with decision that he '''approached''' the problem of his wrecked shop.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[bring up]] or [[propose]] to (someone) an [[idea]], [[question#Noun|question]], [[request#Noun|request]], etc.
# {{lb|en|transitive|archaic|euphemistic}} To have [[sexual intercourse]] with (someone).
#: {{synonyms|en|Thesaurus:copulate}}
#* {{RQ:King James Version|Leviticus|18|6|page=205|column=1|passage=None of you ſhall '''approche''' to any that is neere of kinne to him, to vncouer ''their'' nakedneſſe: I ''am'' the {{smallcaps|Lord}}.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|military}} To [[take#Verb|take]] [[approaches#Noun|approaches]] to (a place); to move towards (a place) by [[use#Verb|using]] [[covered#Adjective|covered]] [[road#Noun|roads]], [[trench#Noun|trenches]], or other [[work#Noun|works]].

=====Usage notes=====
Regarding the use of sense 7 in discussing convergence in mathematical analysis, modern rigorous formulations avoid using the words ''approach'' and {{m|en|converge}}. However, the terms are used informally when rigour is not required.

