===Etymology 1===
{{etymon|en|:inh|enm:complement<ety:der<la:complēmentum>>|id=complete|tree=1}}
From {{inh|en|enm|complement}}, from {{der|en|la|complēmentum||that which fills up or completes}}, from {{m|la|compleō||to fill up; to complete}} (English {{m|en|complete}}). {{doublet|en|compliment}}. The verb is from the noun.<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=v|id=1166019843}}</ref>

====Noun====
{{en-noun|~}}

# The [[totality]], the full amount or number which [[complete]]s something. {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
#* {{RQ:Melville Moby-Dick|text=Queequeg sought a passage to Christian lands. But the ship, having her full '''complement''' of seamen, spurned his suit; and not all the King his father's influence could prevail.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=30 October 2009|journal=The Guardian
|passage=Some 11 members of Somerton council's '''complement''' of 15 stepped down on Tuesday.}}
# {{lb|en|nautical}} The whole working force of a vessel.
# {{lb|en|astronomy|geometry}} An [[angle]] which, together with a given angle, makes a [[right angle]]. {{defdate|from 18th c.}}
# Something which [[complete]]s, something which combines with something else to make up a complete whole; loosely, something perceived to be a harmonious or desirable partner or addition. {{defdate|from 19th c.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1854|author={{w|James Stephen (civil servant)|James Stephen}}|title=On Desultory and Systematic Reading
|passage=History is the '''complement''' of poetry.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1962|month=October|author=Brian Haresnape|title=Focus on B.R. passenger stations|journal=Modern Railways|page=255|text=It would be too much to imagine that improving stations will alone create a much needed new image in the public eye; a smartly turned out station staff is a very necessary '''complement''' to a smart station.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=13 December 2009|journal=The Guardian
|passage=London's Kings Place, now one year old, established itself as a venue for imaginative programming, a '''complement''' to the evergreen Wigmore Hall.}}
# {{senseid|en|grammar}} {{lb|en|grammar|linguistics}} A [[word]] or group of words that completes a [[grammatical]] construction in the [[predicate]] and that describes or is identified with the [[subject]] or [[object]]. {{defdate|from 19th c.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1988|author=Andrew Radford|title=Transformational grammar: a first course|location=Cambridge, UK|publisher=Cambridge University Press|page=340|chapter=7
|passage=Why has our grammar broken down at this point? It is not difficult to see why. For, we have failed to make any provision for the fact that only ''some'' Verbs in English (i.e. Verbs like those italicized in (5) (a), traditionally called ''Transitive Verbs'') subcategorize ( = ‘takeʼ) an immediately following NP '''Complement''', whereas others (such as those italicised in (5) (b), traditionally referred to as ''Intransitive Verbs'') do not.}}
# {{lb|en|paleography|phonetics}} A [[w:phonetic complement|phonetic complement]] is a graphic element that modifies another, such as (in [[Linear B]] script) a small [[syllabogram]] that is attached to a [[logogram]] as an abbreviation of its reading (as opposed to an [[adjunct]] that abbreviates an adjective that modifies that logogram).
# {{lb|en|music}} An [[interval]] which, together with the given interval, makes an [[octave]]. {{defdate|from 19th c.}}
# {{lb|en|optics}} The [[color]] which, when mixed with the given color, gives black (for mixing pigments) or white (for mixing light). {{defdate|from 19th c.}}
#: {{ux|en|The '''complement''' of blue is orange.}}
# {{lb|en|set theory}} Given two sets, the [[set]] containing one set's elements that are not members of the other set (whether a [[relative complement]] or an [[absolute complement]]). {{defdate|from 20th c.}}
#: {{ux|en|The '''complement''' of the odd numbers is the even numbers, relative to the natural numbers.}}
# {{lb|en|immunology}} One of several [[blood]] [[protein]]s that work with [[antibody|antibodies]] during an [[immune response]]. {{defdate|from 20th c.}}
# {{senseid|en|logic}}{{lb|en|logic}} An [[expression]] related to some other expression such that it is [[true]] under the same conditions that make other [[false]], and vice versa. {{defdate|from 20th c.}}
# {{lb|en|electronics}} A [[voltage]] level with the opposite [[logical]] sense to the given one.
# {{lb|en|computing}} A [[bit]] with the opposite value to the given one; the [[logical complement]] of a number.
# {{lb|en|computing|mathematics}} The [[diminished radix complement]] of a number; the [[nines' complement]] of a decimal number; the [[ones' complement]] of a binary number.
#: {{ux|en|The '''complement''' of <math>01100101_2</math> is <math>10011010_2</math>.}}
# {{lb|en|computing|mathematics}} The [[radix complement]] of a number; the [[two's complement]] of a binary number.
#: {{ux|en|The '''complement''' of <math>01100101_2</math> is <math>10011011_2</math>.}}
# {{lb|en|computing|mathematics}} The [[numeric complement]] of a number.
#: {{ux|en|The '''complement''' of &minus;123 is 123.}}
# {{lb|en|genetics}} A [[nucleotide]] sequence in which each base is replaced by the complementary base of the given sequence: [[adenine]] (A) by [[thymine]] (T) or [[uracil]] (U), [[cytosine]] (C) by [[guanine]] (G), and vice versa.
#: {{ux|en|A DNA molecule is formed from two strands, each of which is the '''complement''' of the other.}}
# {{lb|en|biochemistry}} {{synonym of|en|alexin}}.
# {{lb|en|economics}} {{abbr of|en|complementary good}}.
# {{lb|en|now|_|rare}} Something (or someone) that [[complete]]s; the [[consummation]]. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
#* {{RQ:Burton Melancholy|edition=2nd|text=perform all those works of mercy, which Clemens Alexandrinus calls ''amoris et amicitiæ impletionem et extentionem'', the extent and '''complement''' of love{{nb...}}.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} The act of [[completing]] something, or the fact of being [[complete]]; [[completion]], [[completeness]], [[fulfilment]]. {{defdate|15th|18th c.}}
#* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|passage=And both encreast the prayse of woman kynde, / And both encreast her beautie excellent: / So all did make in her a perfect '''complement'''.|3|5}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} Something which completes one's equipment, dress etc.; an [[accessory]]. {{defdate|16th|17th c.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1591|author=w:Edmund Spenser|title=The Teares of the Muses [The Tears of the Muses]: Polyhymnia
|passage=A doleful case desires a doleful song,<br>Without vain art or curious '''complements.'''}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 5|2|2|text=Garnish'd and deck'd in modest complement,}}
#* {{RQ:Montaigne Florio Essayes|chapter=42|book=I|passage=A man should be judged by himselfe, and not by his '''complements'''.}}

